<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khyber Pass</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khyber Pass is a mountain pass linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> with the plains of the north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. It has long been a key route for armies and trade entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> used it during his invasion of India, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> extended its control towards the pass in the nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khyber-pass.jpg" alt="Khyber Pass" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Mollison · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KhyberPassPakistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Khyber Pass lies in the Khyber District of the Pakistani province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">Khyber Pakhtunkhwa</a>, along the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. It connects the town of Landi Kotal to the Valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> at Jamrud, crossing part of the Spin Ghar mountains. The pass proper is about 40 kilometres long, and its summit at Landi Kotal, 1,028 metres high, lies 5 kilometres inside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Pashtun clans, particularly the Afridis and the Afghan Shinwari, have regarded it as their own preserve for centuries and have levied a toll on travellers for safe conduct, which was long their main source of income.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Kushan era the pass was not widely used for trade. Armies of the Achaemenid and Sassanid empires crossed it, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>, as did nomadic invaders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> such as the Saka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> and White Huns. Indian empires rarely extended their control beyond it, with the Maurya king Chandragupta an exception. Greek influence passed through it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> spread the other way. As part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> it carried trade, and Gandhara became a regional centre connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagram">Bagram</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a> in India.</p><p>In the Islamic period Mahmud Ghaznavi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> used the Khyber and nearby passes to invade the subcontinent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> tried to control it but met resistance from local tribes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> was the last major conqueror to cross it. In the 19th century the British East India Company wished to secure the pass against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>. Sikhs under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> captured it in 1837, and their general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh-nalwa">Hari Singh Nalwa</a> built Jamrud Fort to guard it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war">Second Anglo-Afghan War</a> ended in 1880 the region came under British control, and the British paid local tribes to keep the route secure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The British built roads, railways and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> lines through the pass. The Khyber Pass Railway, heavily engineered, runs from Jamrud to the Afghan border near Landi Kotal and opened in 1925. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> concrete dragon's teeth were set on the valley floor out of fear of an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. A common phrase of the colonial period described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> as stretching from Khyber to Kanyakumari. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> in 1947 the pass became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Monuments left by British Indian Army units and hillside forts can still be seen from the road.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khyber Pass” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Forcing the <b>Khyber Pass</b> in late 1738, he took Peshawar, crossed the Indus, occupied Lahore and, after Karnal, entered Delhi in March 1739.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The occupation of Peshawar in 1834 extended the empire to the mouth of the <b>Khyber Pass</b>, giving Ranjit Singh a frontier with Afghanistan and a buffer against Afghan resurgence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
